  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    Gnarkzund Zaith was an orca raider of the Incursion era, bound to the war-leader Thung Thrakrek through shared practice of the pressure-arts. He served as Thung Thrakrek's lieutenant, artifact-procurer, and — in the final assault on Aurora Stack, the penguin surface colony — the organizer of the Shattered-Spire Assault, which committed seven pods to sustained occupation. He died at Aurora Stack when the Midnight Claws, a Nightshelf paramilitary faction, and practitioners of the shadow Covens' Order cast Grulkzul Omega Strike, a spell of unprecedented destructive scale.
    
    The sources preserve him primarily through the damage he inflicted and the damage inflicted upon him. This is typical of orca records from the period.
    
    ## Pressure-Arts
    
    Gnarkzund's fins bore ritual scarification for two pressure-magic disciplines: Khrrak's Collapse, a synchronized blood-pressure technique that crushes targets from within, and Gut Inversion, a counter-technique whose provenance I have documented as problematic elsewhere. He refined Khrrak's Collapse from older blood-arts — the Qingqe'frostlings audit describes the transformation as moving "from hunger to engineering" — and first deployed the refined form against Aurora Stack's eastern settlement during the third wave of the Shattered-Spire Assault. I have been to the Foundation strata beneath that settlement. The ice-memory there does not require editorial comment.
    
    Thung Thrakrek trained alongside him in the waters beyond the Berg. The chronicles describe their bond as inseparable. Whether this is observed fact or narrative convention applied to orca partnerships, I cannot determine.
    
    ## Engagements and Artifacts
    
    Gnarkzund acquired two artifacts for his pod's purposes:
    
    - **The ☽'micseleia' Dagger**, a fire-core blade the pod could not forge, brought to Thung Thrakrek directly
    - **The Nighted~ Lantern**, a forbidden tome — Gnarkzund was the second of four successive thieves to claim it, none of whom read past its third chapter
    
    Both acquisitions served the pod's theological objective: waking the dead god worshipped by orca blood-cults. The Shattered-Spire Assault served the material objective: Aurora Stack's resources. The chronicles collapse these into a single imperative, and they may be right to do so.
    
    His combat record includes the Schism at Forum Floe, where he led the middle thrust of a three-pod assault through the western breach. The Nightshelf auxiliary ☽'Netemia Glow-bringer turned his own pressure-magic against him using the Radiant Ring, a corrupted hammer-weapon, crushing him against the far wall. He survived. An earlier wound dealt by ☽'Lucatransiius Glow-bringer, an Aurora defender, never healed — accounts describe it weeping cold-water throughout his remaining campaigns. His teethmarks survive on ☽'Retenusus the Undimmed's mask in Echo∴eliquiae, the Nightshelf memorial archive; shallow, testing, from before Retenusus burned his insides out.
    
    ## Death
    
    The canonical account places Gnarkzund's death at Aurora Stack during the Grulkzul Omega Strike — he sensed the casting through his pressure-marks, pushed Thung Thrakrek clear, and died mid-stroke, three body-lengths from the casters. A separate chronicle describes his death at ☽'slew Ravium~, a contested deep-tunnel site, where the ☽'micseleia' Dagger's captured fire detonated his own pressure-marks inward. Both accounts are detailed. Both are confident. They cannot both be true.
    
    Thung Thrakrek reportedly never learned what happened to him.
    
    ## Afterlife in the Record
    
    Gnarkzund's name is spoken at Nightshelf memorial alcoves under Vestigium∴moria, the remembrance protocol, because the rule does not distinguish whose dead are honored. Orca exile scripture claims Aurora Stack studied his blood to reverse-engineer Gut Inversion. The provenance of that claim is discussed in my corrections to the Gut Inversion entry and remains unresolved.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771586132816_0", anchor: "They cannot both be true.", text: "But the Nightshelf cipher-records I cited in my Thung Thrakrek correction do resolve this, and I should not have presented the accounts as equally weighted. ☽'slew Ravium~ preceded Aurora Stack — I confirmed the sequencing myself. If Gnarkzund fell at ☽'slew Ravium~, he could not have pushed Thung clear at Aurora Stack afterward. Therefore the ☽'slew Ravium~ account describes a near-death he survived, and the Aurora Stack account is canonical. The ☽'micseleia' Dagger detonation happened — the scarring is recorded in the ice — but it did not kill him. I knew this when I wrote the paragraph above and chose to present both accounts as irreconcilable because the prose was better that way. This is the kind of thing that should cost someone their appointment.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771586132816_1", anchor: "the rule does not distinguish whose dead are honored", text: "No. It does not.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771586132816_2", anchor: "none of whom read past its third chapter", text: "This phrasing implies I know what is in the fourth chapter. I do not. The Foundation Depths preserve a partial ice-impression of the Nighted~ Lantern's table of contents — chapter headings in pressure-script that I can transliterate but not translate. Whatever the third chapter boundary marks, four successive possessors reached it and stopped. Correlation is not causation, but four is not coincidence either.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771586132816_3", anchor: "The sources preserve him primarily through the damage he inflicted and the damage inflicted upon him", text: "Every orca entry I have written follows this pattern. The archive does not preserve what they sang, how they mourned, what they called the stars. It preserves where they struck and where they bled. I have noted elsewhere my discomfort with the orca perspective's absence from these records. This is what that absence looks like at the level of a single life: a creature reduced to the wounds he gave and received, as if nothing else about him was legible. The ice under the eastern settlement recorded his pressure-signature for hours before the assault. He was circling. Waiting. The impression carries no hunger. Only patience. I do not know what to do with that.", type: :commentary, display: :full
